The create_model function will
define and compile our model.

00:12:52.090 --> 00:12:55.320
And this is using the Keras
functional API to do so.

00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:58.050
If you're not very familiar
with TensorFlow in Keras,

00:12:58.050 --> 00:12:59.500
don't worry too much about it.

00:12:59.500 --> 00:13:02.370
This function is really just
here to create the model.

00:13:02.370 --> 00:13:05.100
And whatever framework
you're using,

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:07.050
just think about that
in context of how

00:13:07.050 --> 00:13:10.920
you would create a model in
that particular framework.

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:15.390
Now the main function is, of
course, where most of the stuff

00:13:15.390 --> 00:13:16.300
is happening.

00:13:16.300 --> 00:13:20.670
So the first thing we do
here is we get our args

00:13:20.670 --> 00:13:24.460
and we then create our
distribution strategy.

00:13:24.460 --> 00:13:26.880
So again, there are kind
of two high-level concepts

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:28.020
we're working with here--

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:30.540
distributed training and
hyperparameter tuning.

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:32.820
So in order to do
distributed training,

00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:35.760
the first thing you need to
do is define our distribution

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:36.550
strategy.

00:13:36.550 --> 00:13:40.530
And as I mentioned earlier,
we are using the TensorFlow

00:13:40.530 --> 00:13:41.250
MirroredStrategy.

00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:43.050
So we define that
right over here.

00:13:43.050 --> 00:13:47.280
The next step is to scale
our batch size by the number

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:48.960
of GPUs that we have.

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:52.170
So that's what's happening
in this line right over here.

00:13:52.170 --> 00:13:54.570
Instead of setting the
number of GPUs to two

00:13:54.570 --> 00:13:56.250
and just hardcoding
it right here,

00:13:56.250 --> 00:13:59.920
I went ahead and used this
num_replicas_in_sync method,

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:02.580
which will just return
the number of GPUs

00:14:02.580 --> 00:14:04.530
that we have on our machine.

00:14:04.530 --> 00:14:07.170
So that's going to be two
when we launch our training

00:14:07.170 --> 00:14:09.360
job because we're going
to make use of two GPUs,

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:11.580
but this just saves us
from having to hardcode

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:12.930
that number in here.

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:14.910
And the reason we
scale the batch size

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:18.330
is now, because we have two
GPUs or we have multiple GPUs,

00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:20.910
we want to make sure that
we're using every GPU

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:22.630
to its maximum potential.

00:14:22.630 --> 00:14:25.320
So scaling up the batch
size is a best practice

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:27.330
when you use the
MirroredStrategy

00:14:27.330 --> 00:14:29.405
from the tf.distribute module.

00:14:29.405 --> 00:14:31.530
And again, if you'd like
to learn a little bit more

00:14:31.530 --> 00:14:34.290
about why that is the
case, be sure to check out

00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:36.810
the video which I have
linked in the Training Code

00:14:36.810 --> 00:14:39.580
section of the codelab as well.

00:14:39.580 --> 00:14:42.630
And then the last step
you'll need for tf.distribute

00:14:42.630 --> 00:14:45.930
is to wrap the creation
of your model variables

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:47.950
within the scope
of the strategy.

00:14:47.950 --> 00:14:50.010
So that's what's
happening in line 89.

00:14:50.010 --> 00:14:53.460
We call with
strategy.scope, and then we

00:14:53.460 --> 00:14:55.560
call the create_model function.

00:14:55.560 --> 00:14:57.240
And again, the
create_model function

00:14:57.240 --> 00:15:00.210
is what creates the model
and also compiles it.

00:15:00.210 --> 00:15:03.183
So that's where all of our
model variables get created.

00:15:03.183 --> 00:15:05.100
And that's all you need
to do in order to make

00:15:05.100 --> 00:15:07.050
use of distributed training.

00:15:07.050 --> 00:15:09.960
This now means that, when
our model training kicks off,

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:14.860
it will be able to use multiple
GPUs in order to train faster.

00:15:14.860 --> 00:15:17.430
So the next important
things we need to do

00:15:17.430 --> 00:15:20.640
are make sure that our code is
prepared to do hyperparameter

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:22.352
training with Vertex AI.

00:15:22.352 --> 00:15:24.060
So the first thing
that you'll want to do

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:28.320
is, all the way up at the top
where we import the Hypertune

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:29.370
library.

00:15:29.370 --> 00:15:31.680
So this library
here basically helps

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:35.790
us to report the metrics that
we care about to Vertex AI.

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:37.890
And recall, earlier,
I showed you

00:15:37.890 --> 00:15:40.830
what that end state looks like
where we have multiple training

00:15:40.830 --> 00:15:43.440
trials and we were
recording the accuracy

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:45.090
of those different trials.

00:15:45.090 --> 00:15:48.490
This Hypertune library is
what's going to help us do that.

00:15:48.490 --> 00:15:51.090
So after you've imported
the Hypertune library,

00:15:51.090 --> 00:15:55.530
the next thing you need to do is
create this get_args function.

00:15:55.530 --> 00:15:58.050
And you'll use
argparse to define

00:15:58.050 --> 00:16:00.960
all of the hyperparameters
that you want to tune.

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:03.840
So in this case, we have
chosen to tune the learning

00:16:03.840 --> 00:16:07.080
rate, the momentum,
and the number of units

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:08.640
in the last hidden layer.

00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:10.830
And these are just
three hyperparameters

00:16:10.830 --> 00:16:14.940
that I chose to tune, but feel
free to experiment with others

00:16:14.940 --> 00:16:18.450
or add more, change these
out, whatever you're

00:16:18.450 --> 00:16:20.640
curious to experiment with.

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:23.910
Something to note is that you
can call these hyperparameters

00:16:23.910 --> 00:16:25.380
whatever you like.

00:16:25.380 --> 00:16:28.470
I chose learning rate,
momentum, num_units.

00:16:28.470 --> 00:16:29.940
These string values
are up to you

00:16:29.940 --> 00:16:32.490
to decide just need to make
sure that you are consistent

00:16:32.490 --> 00:16:33.407
everywhere.

00:16:33.407 --> 00:16:35.490
When you launch the job,
you'll want to refer back

00:16:35.490 --> 00:16:37.380
to these strings as well.

00:16:37.380 --> 00:16:38.880
So just make sure
you're consistent,

00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:41.205
but otherwise you can call
them whatever you like.

00:16:41.205 --> 00:16:43.080
Now, the next thing
we'll need to do in order

00:16:43.080 --> 00:16:45.570
to use the hyperparameter
tuning service

00:16:45.570 --> 00:16:47.580
is to define our metric.

00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:50.250
So that's what's happening
over here and line 96.

00:16:50.250 --> 00:16:53.490
It's important that we have
a metric that we are tracking

00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:55.830
because we want to
know how effective

00:16:55.830 --> 00:16:59.070
each trial of our
hyperparameter tuning job--

00:16:59.070 --> 00:17:01.080
how effective each trial was.

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:03.600
If we just were to
run multiple trials

00:17:03.600 --> 00:17:05.550
but not be tracking
a metric, we would

00:17:05.550 --> 00:17:09.220
have no idea which trial
was the most performant.

00:17:09.220 --> 00:17:12.450
So in this case, I've decided
that validation accuracy is

00:17:12.450 --> 00:17:15.990
the metric that I want to
track, but it might be different

00:17:15.990 --> 00:17:18.990
for, of course, the regression
problem or maybe a problem

00:17:18.990 --> 00:17:20.250
with class imbalance.

00:17:20.250 --> 00:17:22.290
Whatever metric it
is that you think

00:17:22.290 --> 00:17:25.410
is relevant to your problem,
you'll want to define that.

00:17:25.410 --> 00:17:27.960
And then I've defined that
as this HP metric right here.

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:29.760
Now, this might look
a little strange,

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:32.310
but this is just something
that's particular to Keras.

00:17:32.310 --> 00:17:35.880
When you use model.fit in
Keras, as we have here,

00:17:35.880 --> 00:17:38.370
it will return this
history object.

00:17:38.370 --> 00:17:41.160
And the history.history
attribute

00:17:41.160 --> 00:17:45.060
is a record of the training
loss values and metric values

00:17:45.060 --> 00:17:47.040
at successive epochs.

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:48.860
So when we compiled
the model, we

00:17:48.860 --> 00:17:51.360
passed in this accuracy
metric right here.

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:54.620
And that just means that our
history.history attribute

00:17:54.620 --> 00:17:57.500
is going to keep a
record of both the loss

00:17:57.500 --> 00:18:00.080
as well as the accuracy
for our training

00:18:00.080 --> 00:18:03.150
and our validation data
sets on each epoch.

00:18:03.150 --> 00:18:07.850
So here I'm just extracting
out the validation accuracy key

00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:11.210
and I'm pulling the last
value from that list

00:18:11.210 --> 00:18:14.420
because we want the last
epoch's accuracy measure.

00:18:14.420 --> 00:18:18.290
If you're using a different
framework such as Scikit-learn.

00:18:18.290 --> 00:18:21.390
And I think Scikit-learn has
that accuracy score function.

00:18:21.390 --> 00:18:23.220
So that's really what
you would use here.

00:18:23.220 --> 00:18:27.410
This is just your calculation of
the metric that you care about.

00:18:27.410 --> 00:18:30.652
And once you have defined
and calculated that metric--

00:18:30.652 --> 00:18:33.110
and again, in this case, we're
interested in the validation

00:18:33.110 --> 00:18:34.580
accuracy--

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:37.880
we will go ahead and create
an instance of Hypertune

00:18:37.880 --> 00:18:39.950
using this Hypertune library.

00:18:39.950 --> 00:18:43.400
And then we will pass
in a tag for our metric.

00:18:43.400 --> 00:18:45.050
We're going to call it accuracy.

00:18:45.050 --> 00:18:48.630
And then we will also pass
in the actual metric value,

00:18:48.630 --> 00:18:50.940
which we defined as HP metric.

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:54.500
So what this means is that each
time our training code is run,

00:18:54.500 --> 00:18:57.570
we'll have this model.fit,
it'll train the model,

00:18:57.570 --> 00:18:59.540
and then, after the
model has been trained,

00:18:59.540 --> 00:19:02.150
we're capturing what the
validation accuracy looked

00:19:02.150 --> 00:19:06.950
like on the last epoch and we're
reporting that to Vertex AI

00:19:06.950 --> 00:19:09.290
so that each time we run one
of these training trials,

00:19:09.290 --> 00:19:12.560
we're able to see what the
validation accuracy was

00:19:12.560 --> 00:19:15.800
and that will help us determine
which trial performed the best.

00:19:15.800 --> 00:19:19.610
With that, we are ready to
go ahead and containerize

00:19:19.610 --> 00:19:20.670
our code.

00:19:20.670 --> 00:19:23.150
So I'm going to jump back
over to the terminal,

00:19:23.150 --> 00:19:26.900
and from the root of our
Vertex codelab directory,

00:19:26.900 --> 00:19:29.750
I will create a Docker file.

00:19:29.750 --> 00:19:33.050
So we can see that Docker
file right over here.

00:19:33.050 --> 00:19:37.640
And I'm going to paste in
the following commands.

00:19:37.640 --> 00:19:39.680
And what this file
is doing here is

00:19:39.680 --> 00:19:41.840
it's using, as the
base image over here

00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:46.250
on line 1, the deep learning
container TensorFlow Enterprise

00:19:46.250 --> 00:19:48.770
2.7 GPU Docker image.

00:19:48.770 --> 00:19:51.140
These deep learning
containers on Google Cloud

00:19:51.140 --> 00:19:53.870
come with many common machine
learning and data science

00:19:53.870 --> 00:19:55.670
frameworks pre-installed.

00:19:55.670 --> 00:19:58.310
And then, after
downloading that image,

00:19:58.310 --> 00:20:02.720
we are going to pip install
the Cloud ML Hypertune library.

00:20:02.720 --> 00:20:05.810
This is the library Hypertune
that we import in our script

00:20:05.810 --> 00:20:08.660
so you just need to make sure
that you install this library

00:20:08.660 --> 00:20:11.480
if you are using a base
image that doesn't already

00:20:11.480 --> 00:20:14.690
come with this
library pre-installed

00:20:14.690 --> 00:20:17.900
and then, in line 9, we're
copying over all this training

00:20:17.900 --> 00:20:20.970
code to our Docker image,
and then in line 12,

00:20:20.970 --> 00:20:23.430
we will set up the entry
point to invoke our trainer.

00:20:23.430 --> 00:20:25.640
And all this means
is that it's going

00:20:25.640 --> 00:20:29.420
to run our task.py file,
which is the file where we

00:20:29.420 --> 00:20:30.860
had all of our training code.

00:20:30.860 --> 00:20:34.310
So back in the terminal, we can
now go ahead and build and push

00:20:34.310 --> 00:20:35.300
the container.

00:20:35.300 --> 00:20:36.800
So the first thing
you'll need to do

00:20:36.800 --> 00:20:39.650
is define a variable
for your project ID.

00:20:39.650 --> 00:20:42.005
Now, I happen to know
what my project ID is.

00:20:47.530 --> 00:20:49.230
However, if you
do not know, there

00:20:49.230 --> 00:20:52.920
is a handy command that's
listed in the instructions

00:20:52.920 --> 00:20:54.970
in the codelab.

00:20:54.970 --> 00:20:57.910
The next thing we'll want
to do is define a variable

00:20:57.910 --> 00:21:01.270
with the UI of the container
image in Google Container

00:21:01.270 --> 00:21:01.990
Registry.

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:04.780
And this is where we
will push our image.

00:21:04.780 --> 00:21:08.050
Container Registry is
the place in Google Cloud

00:21:08.050 --> 00:21:12.440
where you can store any
of your Docker images.

00:21:12.440 --> 00:21:14.920
The next command we'll
run is this gcloud auth

00:21:14.920 --> 00:21:16.750
configure-docker,
which will just

00:21:16.750 --> 00:21:19.570
help us to configure
usage of Docker.

00:21:19.570 --> 00:21:21.910
And then we can go ahead
and build our image.

00:21:25.470 --> 00:21:27.960
And this will take a
few minutes, probably

00:21:27.960 --> 00:21:30.450
about five or so minutes,
the very first time

00:21:30.450 --> 00:21:31.245
that you do this.

00:21:31.245 --> 00:21:33.120
It's going to work a
little bit faster for me

00:21:33.120 --> 00:21:34.650
because I have done this before.

00:21:34.650 --> 00:21:37.420
But note that the first time
you do run this command,

00:21:37.420 --> 00:21:38.955
it will take a
little bit of time.

00:21:45.230 --> 00:21:47.690
OK, our image has
been built. And now

00:21:47.690 --> 00:21:52.850
the next step is to push this
image to a container registry.

00:21:52.850 --> 00:21:54.680
So we'll call docker push.

00:21:54.680 --> 00:21:56.790
And again, this will
take a little bit of time

00:21:56.790 --> 00:21:58.040
the very first time you do it.

00:21:58.040 --> 00:22:02.220
I've done this before
so it was pretty fast.

00:22:02.220 --> 00:22:04.830
All right, so the last step
before we go ahead and launch

00:22:04.830 --> 00:22:07.860
the hyperparameter tuning job
is to create a Cloud Storage

00:22:07.860 --> 00:22:08.490
bucket.

00:22:08.490 --> 00:22:10.290
We will just need
a staging bucket

00:22:10.290 --> 00:22:12.550
for when we launch
our training job.

00:22:12.550 --> 00:22:14.580
So we can go ahead
and create our bucket.

00:22:17.590 --> 00:22:20.640
So I've chosen to call the
bucket the name of my project

00:22:20.640 --> 00:22:23.490
ID -hptune-bucket.

00:22:23.490 --> 00:22:25.030
Buckets need to be unique.

00:22:25.030 --> 00:22:26.820
So this is just an
easy way to make

00:22:26.820 --> 00:22:30.595
sure we create a unique bucket.

00:22:30.595 --> 00:22:32.970
And you can create the bucket
with the following command.

00:22:32.970 --> 00:22:35.520
I'm not going to execute this
command because I've already

00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:36.550
created this bucket.

00:22:36.550 --> 00:22:38.400
But if you are
creating a new bucket,

00:22:38.400 --> 00:22:40.180
this command will
help you do that.

00:22:40.180 --> 00:22:42.330
And with that, we are
ready to head over

00:22:42.330 --> 00:22:47.620
to step 7 and launch our
hyperparameter tuning job.

00:22:47.620 --> 00:22:51.000
So the next thing we'll do is
go back and open the launcher

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:52.830
again one more time.

00:22:52.830 --> 00:22:57.120
And I will create a
TensorFlow 2 notebook.

00:22:57.120 --> 00:22:59.280
And in this notebook, the
first thing we need to do

00:22:59.280 --> 00:23:01.800
is import the Vertex
AI Python SDK.

00:23:07.380 --> 00:23:11.910
So we can import this SDK.

00:23:11.910 --> 00:23:16.470
And then we will need to define
a couple of different specs.

00:23:16.470 --> 00:23:18.150
The first spec that
we'll define is

00:23:18.150 --> 00:23:20.610
called the worker
pull specs, and this

00:23:20.610 --> 00:23:22.950
specifies the machine
type and the Docker

00:23:22.950 --> 00:23:24.465
image for our training job.

00:23:28.980 --> 00:23:32.630
So here we're specifying an
n1-standard-4 machine type,

00:23:32.630 --> 00:23:36.470
we're specifying an Nvidia
Tesla V100 as our accelerator,

00:23:36.470 --> 00:23:38.360
and we're going to
use two accelerators.

00:23:38.360 --> 00:23:41.270
And again, this is so that
we have two GPUs so that we

00:23:41.270 --> 00:23:43.550
can do distributed training.

00:23:43.550 --> 00:23:45.920
Feel free to try out
different machine types,

00:23:45.920 --> 00:23:49.730
different accelerator types,
use more or fewer accelerators.

00:23:49.730 --> 00:23:52.280
The more accelerators you use,
of course, the more expensive

00:23:52.280 --> 00:23:54.050
it will be, but feel
free to experiment

00:23:54.050 --> 00:23:55.580
here however you like.

00:23:55.580 --> 00:23:59.180
And the only thing you'll need
to change is in this image_uri,

00:23:59.180 --> 00:24:01.085
you'll need to add in
your own project ID.

00:24:07.230 --> 00:24:12.170
So there we have our
worker pool spec,

00:24:12.170 --> 00:24:15.060
the next step is to
define the parameter spec.

00:24:15.060 --> 00:24:17.690
And this is a dictionary
specifying the parameters

00:24:17.690 --> 00:24:19.890
that we want to optimize.

00:24:19.890 --> 00:24:22.040
So in this dictionary,
the keys are

00:24:22.040 --> 00:24:25.160
going to be the strings that
we assign to the command line

00:24:25.160 --> 00:24:28.730
argument for each hyperparameter
that we wanted to tune--

00:24:28.730 --> 00:24:31.610
so recall, in our
get_args function earlier,

00:24:31.610 --> 00:24:34.460
we defined these arguments--
learning rate, momentum,

00:24:34.460 --> 00:24:35.480
and num_units.

00:24:35.480 --> 00:24:37.970
And we're just making sure
we use the same strings so

00:24:37.970 --> 00:24:39.380
that we're consistent.

00:24:39.380 --> 00:24:43.610
And the value is going to be
a parameter specification.

00:24:43.610 --> 00:24:46.850
And you can check out the
Vertex AI Python SDK docs

00:24:46.850 --> 00:24:49.190
to learn a little bit more
about these different specs.

00:24:49.190 --> 00:24:51.440
But at a high level,
hyperparameters

00:24:51.440 --> 00:24:55.490
can be of type double, integer,
categorical, or discrete.

00:24:55.490 --> 00:24:57.980
If you select the types
double or integer,

00:24:57.980 --> 00:25:01.520
you'll need to provide a minimum
and maximum value as well

00:25:01.520 --> 00:25:02.930
as a scale.

00:25:02.930 --> 00:25:05.127
I linked, in the
codelab, a video that

00:25:05.127 --> 00:25:06.710
helps you understand
a little bit more

00:25:06.710 --> 00:25:09.650
about how to pick the best
scale in case you're curious.

00:25:09.650 --> 00:25:12.170
And if you select
categorical or discrete,

00:25:12.170 --> 00:25:15.060
you will need to provide
the actual values.

00:25:15.060 --> 00:25:17.540
So in this example,
learning rate and momentum

00:25:17.540 --> 00:25:19.220
are both going to be doubles.

00:25:19.220 --> 00:25:23.600
And I've provided a minimum and
maximum value for each of them.

00:25:23.600 --> 00:25:25.640
And I've set the
learning rate scale

00:25:25.640 --> 00:25:29.540
to be log and the scale
for momentum to be linear.

00:25:29.540 --> 00:25:32.280
And the number of units is
going to just be discrete.

00:25:32.280 --> 00:25:34.490
So I went ahead and
set all of the values.

00:25:34.490 --> 00:25:35.750
You can see them right here--

00:25:35.750 --> 00:25:38.420
64, 128, and 512.

00:25:38.420 --> 00:25:40.790
And that just means one of
these values will get picked

00:25:40.790 --> 00:25:43.040
and the scale is,
of course, none

00:25:43.040 --> 00:25:46.760
because we just are picking
from a discrete list.

00:25:46.760 --> 00:25:48.530
So we can execute the cell.

00:25:51.050 --> 00:25:55.130
And the last step is to
define the metric_spec.

00:25:55.130 --> 00:25:58.070
So this is the last spec,
and this is the metric

00:25:58.070 --> 00:25:59.660
that we want to optimize.

00:25:59.660 --> 00:26:02.450
Again, we were optimizing
the validation accuracy

00:26:02.450 --> 00:26:07.100
and we called that accuracy that
was the string that we defined

00:26:07.100 --> 00:26:10.220
earlier as the
hyperparameter metric tag

00:26:10.220 --> 00:26:12.260
that we set in our
training application code.

00:26:12.260 --> 00:26:14.750
And the value here is going
to be the optimization

00:26:14.750 --> 00:26:15.800
goal of that metric.

00:26:15.800 --> 00:26:18.470
And that can either be
maximized or minimized.

00:26:18.470 --> 00:26:20.752
Of course we want to
maximize accuracy,

00:26:20.752 --> 00:26:23.210
but you might have use cases
where you're tracking a metric

00:26:23.210 --> 00:26:24.320
that you want to minimize.

00:26:24.320 --> 00:26:26.430
Maybe it's loss.

00:26:26.430 --> 00:26:30.440
But in this case, we want
to maximize this metric.

00:26:30.440 --> 00:26:34.050
That's defined all of
the specs that we need.

00:26:34.050 --> 00:26:36.830
The next step is to
define the custom job.

00:26:36.830 --> 00:26:38.210
This is the common
spec that will

00:26:38.210 --> 00:26:41.690
be used to run the job on each
of the hyperparameter tuning

00:26:41.690 --> 00:26:42.500
trials.

00:26:42.500 --> 00:26:44.930
So that means that when
the hyperparameter tuning

00:26:44.930 --> 00:26:49.460
job kicks off, multiple
custom jobs will be run.

00:26:49.460 --> 00:26:51.080
This is taking in
the worker pool

00:26:51.080 --> 00:26:54.830
spec which we defined earlier
where we specified our machine

00:26:54.830 --> 00:26:58.820
as well as the Docker image
that has the training code.

00:26:58.820 --> 00:27:02.120
So you'll need to make sure
that you update your bucket here

00:27:02.120 --> 00:27:05.390
and that you put in the bucket
that we defined earlier.

00:27:05.390 --> 00:27:08.640
This just needs to be some
staging bucket in your project.

00:27:08.640 --> 00:27:09.830
So we'll define that here.

00:27:20.730 --> 00:27:22.810
And then we can
execute the cell.

00:27:22.810 --> 00:27:26.130
The next step is to create and
run our hyperparameter tuning

00:27:26.130 --> 00:27:28.060
job.

00:27:28.060 --> 00:27:32.280
So this hyperparameter tuning
job will pass in the custom job

00:27:32.280 --> 00:27:33.720
that we defined earlier.

00:27:33.720 --> 00:27:37.410
We'll also passed in the metric
spec and the parameter spec.

00:27:37.410 --> 00:27:39.540
And then these last
three arguments

00:27:39.540 --> 00:27:42.840
are specific to running
hyperparameter tuning jobs.

00:27:42.840 --> 00:27:45.090
The first is the
max_trial_count.

00:27:45.090 --> 00:27:47.640
You'll need to put an upper
bound on the number of trials

00:27:47.640 --> 00:27:48.990
that the service will run.

00:27:48.990 --> 00:27:52.050
And generally, more trials
will lead to better results,

00:27:52.050 --> 00:27:54.990
but you will reach a point
of diminishing returns

00:27:54.990 --> 00:27:57.180
after which
additional trials have

00:27:57.180 --> 00:27:59.310
little or no effect
on the metric

00:27:59.310 --> 00:28:01.210
that you're trying to optimize.

00:28:01.210 --> 00:28:04.320
So generally, a best practice
is to start with a smaller

00:28:04.320 --> 00:28:06.630
number of trials,
get a sense for how

00:28:06.630 --> 00:28:08.790
impactful your chosen
hyperparameters are,

00:28:08.790 --> 00:28:10.810
and then scale up as needed.

00:28:10.810 --> 00:28:13.680
The second argument is
the parallel_trial_count.

00:28:13.680 --> 00:28:16.230
And if you choose to
use parallel trials,

00:28:16.230 --> 00:28:19.050
the service will provision
multiple training processing

00:28:19.050 --> 00:28:19.950
clusters.

00:28:19.950 --> 00:28:21.960
Increasing the number
of parallel trials

00:28:21.960 --> 00:28:24.570
will reduce the amount
of time, overall,

00:28:24.570 --> 00:28:27.180
that the hyperparameter
tuning job takes to run.

00:28:27.180 --> 00:28:31.290
However, the downside is that
it can reduce the effectiveness

00:28:31.290 --> 00:28:32.730
of the job overall.

00:28:32.730 --> 00:28:35.550
And that's because the
default tuning strategy

00:28:35.550 --> 00:28:37.560
will use results
of previous trials

00:28:37.560 --> 00:28:41.230
to inform the assignments of
values in subsequent trials.

00:28:41.230 --> 00:28:43.260
So we're defining
that tuning strategy

00:28:43.260 --> 00:28:46.440
here in the search
algorithm argument, which

00:28:46.440 --> 00:28:47.860
we've set to none.

00:28:47.860 --> 00:28:50.670
And that's because, by
default, this default option

00:28:50.670 --> 00:28:52.920
will apply a
Bayesian optimization

00:28:52.920 --> 00:28:57.030
algorithm to search the space of
possible hyperparameter values.

00:28:57.030 --> 00:28:59.340
This is the
recommended algorithm.

00:28:59.340 --> 00:29:02.183
And I have linked to a
blog in the codelab which

00:29:02.183 --> 00:29:04.350
has a little bit more
information on the mathematics

00:29:04.350 --> 00:29:05.550
of how this works.

00:29:05.550 --> 00:29:08.610
If you're curious, it's really
interesting so definitely

00:29:08.610 --> 00:29:10.210
worth checking out.

00:29:10.210 --> 00:29:12.975
So with that, we will launch
our hyperparameter tuning job.

00:29:17.720 --> 00:29:19.750
So now that our
job has kicked off,

00:29:19.750 --> 00:29:23.350
we can head back over to
the Google Cloud Console.

00:29:23.350 --> 00:29:28.330
And we can head to the
Training tab right here.

00:29:28.330 --> 00:29:30.670
And we can go to
hyperparameter tuning jobs

00:29:30.670 --> 00:29:32.830
and we can see that the
job we just kicked off

00:29:32.830 --> 00:29:34.370
is currently running.

00:29:34.370 --> 00:29:37.750
So we can click on this job,
and we won't see a whole lot yet

00:29:37.750 --> 00:29:40.430
because nothing's happened yet.

00:29:40.430 --> 00:29:43.630
But we can see kind of the
start time, when it was created,

00:29:43.630 --> 00:29:45.610
how long it's been running for.

00:29:45.610 --> 00:29:50.380
We can check out the specs
that we defined, all here.

00:29:50.380 --> 00:29:53.650
These are all the specs that
we defined in our notebook.

00:29:53.650 --> 00:29:56.260
And then, eventually,
when the trials are run,

00:29:56.260 --> 00:29:58.820
we'll be able to see
the results down here.

00:29:58.820 --> 00:30:00.820
So this is going to take
about 30 or so minutes.

00:30:00.820 --> 00:30:02.710
So I'm going to hop
back over and we'll just

00:30:02.710 --> 00:30:05.080
take a look at a job that
I've run in the past that

00:30:05.080 --> 00:30:07.270
has already completed.

00:30:07.270 --> 00:30:09.850
And this will be
the same end result.

00:30:09.850 --> 00:30:12.290
And this is what I
showed at the beginning.

00:30:12.290 --> 00:30:15.620
So let's go ahead and see
all of the trials at once.

00:30:15.620 --> 00:30:18.290
And we can go ahead and
sort these by accuracy.

00:30:18.290 --> 00:30:20.260
So what happened
behind the scenes

00:30:20.260 --> 00:30:22.540
is that our training
code that we defined

00:30:22.540 --> 00:30:26.560
in that task.py file, it was
executed six different times

00:30:26.560 --> 00:30:28.810
because we chose to run
six different trials.

00:30:28.810 --> 00:30:31.600
Each of those trials tried
a different learning rate,

00:30:31.600 --> 00:30:33.470
momentum, and number of units.

00:30:33.470 --> 00:30:35.440
And you can see the
combinations that

00:30:35.440 --> 00:30:37.240
were selected right over here.

00:30:37.240 --> 00:30:41.530
And on the 10th epoch, which was
the final epoch of our training

00:30:41.530 --> 00:30:44.170
job, we can see the
validation accuracy

00:30:44.170 --> 00:30:45.480
score that was reported.

00:30:45.480 --> 00:30:47.980
We of course want the trial
that led to the highest accuracy

00:30:47.980 --> 00:30:48.830
score.

00:30:48.830 --> 00:30:50.770
So that's trial ID 5.

00:30:50.770 --> 00:30:53.680
And it had the following
learning rate momentum

00:30:53.680 --> 00:30:54.820
and number of units.

00:30:54.820 --> 00:30:57.070
So if 0.82 was a
good enough accuracy

00:30:57.070 --> 00:30:59.360
score for our
particular use case,

00:30:59.360 --> 00:31:03.020
we could go ahead and set these
hyperparameters to the values

00:31:03.020 --> 00:31:03.520
here.

00:31:03.520 --> 00:31:05.590
If we wanted to aim
for higher accuracy,

00:31:05.590 --> 00:31:08.650
we could go ahead and run
even more trials since 6

00:31:08.650 --> 00:31:10.960
is a pretty low number
and see if we can do

00:31:10.960 --> 00:31:12.550
even better on this data set.

00:31:12.550 --> 00:31:16.660
And one other last note is that
you can actually do all of this

00:31:16.660 --> 00:31:18.290
through the UI.

00:31:18.290 --> 00:31:19.990
So if we look back
over to Training,

00:31:19.990 --> 00:31:22.210
you can click on this
Create button here

00:31:22.210 --> 00:31:25.750
and you can actually
create a training job.

00:31:25.750 --> 00:31:28.570
And under step 4 here,
you'll have the option

00:31:28.570 --> 00:31:30.680
to set your hyperparameters.

00:31:30.680 --> 00:31:33.280
So if you prefer to
do this through a UI

00:31:33.280 --> 00:31:38.130
instead of doing this via the
SDK, that is also an option.

00:31:38.130 --> 00:31:40.530
So that concludes the
workshop for today.

